\section{Introduction}
Model Predictive Control (MPC) is a widely used optimal control technique known for its ability to handle multivariable systems and constraints systematically. At each time step, MPC solves a finite-horizon optimization problem to compute control actions, which are then applied in a receding horizon fashion. However, one of the fundamental limitations of conventional MPC approaches is their dependence on accurate mathematical models of the underlying system dynamics. Obtaining such models through first-principles derivation or classical system identification techniques can be time-consuming, error-prone, and infeasible for complex or poorly understood systems.

To address these challenges, recent research has shifted toward data-driven control approaches, which aim to leverage measured data directly for control design, bypassing the explicit modeling step. Among these, a particularly promising framework is the one introduced by \cite{berberich2021}, which formulates an MPC scheme based purely on measured input-output trajectories without relying on a parametric model.

This data-driven MPC framework utilizes concepts from behavioral systems theory, particularly Willems' Fundamental Lemma, to express system trajectories as linear combinations of past data sequences. Under the assumption that the input is persistently exciting, it is possible to reconstruct future behavior of the system directly from historical data using Hankel matrices. This eliminates the need for an intermediate model identification step and enables the implementation of MPC with theoretical guarantees on stability and robustness.

The objective of this internship was to gain practical understanding of such data-driven control methods through a structured implementation of the aforementioned MPC framework. The internship involved in-depth exploration of the theoretical foundations, careful replication of the proposed algorithms using Python and CasADi, and evaluation of the control performance on a benchmark four-tank system. Rather than contributing novel research, the focus was on accurately reproducing and understanding the work of \cite{berberich2021}, thereby solidifying core competencies in advanced control and optimization.

In the following sections, we present the internship objectives, the theoretical background of the method, details of the implementation, simulation results, challenges encountered, and concluding remarks on the learning outcomes and potential future work.



\section{Internship Objectives}

The primary aim of this internship was to gain hands-on experience with modern control methodologies that leverage data rather than explicit mathematical models. The tasks were designed to develop both theoretical understanding and practical implementation skills aligned with the framework introduced by \cite{berberich2021}. The internship comprised the following structured objectives:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Familiarization with CasADi for Optimization:} Gain proficiency in using CasADi, a symbolic framework for numerical optimization in Python, with a specific focus on constructing and solving nonlinear and quadratic programming problems relevant to MPC.

\item \textbf{System Identification Using Data-Driven Methods:} Implement data acquisition routines to generate persistently exciting input-output trajectories. Use these trajectories to construct Hankel matrices as required by the behavioral systems theory framework.

\item \textbf{Implementation of Data-Driven MPC Algorithms:} Reproduce the nominal and robust data-driven MPC schemes described by Berberich et al., including terminal constraints and the handling of bounded measurement noise through slack variables and regularization terms.

\item \textbf{Simulation on a Benchmark System:} Simulate the implemented controllers on a discretized linear model of the four-tank system. This includes applying the controller in a closed-loop setting and analyzing its ability to track reference outputs and maintain robustness against disturbances.

\end{enumerate}

Each objective was pursued in a step-by-step manner, reinforcing both theoretical knowledge and implementation capability. The following sections of this report detail the methods used and the insights gained in the course of meeting these objectives.


\section{Theoretical Background}

This section presents the theoretical underpinnings of the data-driven MPC scheme explored during the internship. All material in this section is directly grounded in the work of \cite{berberich2021} and \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop} with no extrapolations or modifications.

\subsection{Behavioral Systems Perspective}
Traditional control methods typically rely on an explicit model of the system dynamics, either derived from first principles or obtained through parameter estimation. The data-driven framework employed in this internship takes a fundamentally different perspective based on behavioral systems theory. Rather than modeling system states or parameters, it represents the set of all possible input-output trajectories as a data-driven linear space.

A key result from this theory is that if the input trajectory used to collect data is persistently exciting of a sufficiently high order, then all future system trajectories can be represented as linear combinations of measured data. This result enables a model-free representation of the system suitable for prediction and control.

\subsection{Hankel Matrix Construction}
Given an input-output trajectory \[
\left\{ u_k, y_k \right\}_{k=0}^{N-1}
\] from an unknown discrete-time linear time-invariant (LTI) system, the approach constructs a block Hankel matrix of the form:
\begin{equation}
H_L(u_d, y_d) = \begin{bmatrix} H_L(u_d) \\ H_L(y_d) \end{bmatrix},
\end{equation}
where $H_L(u_d)$ and $H_L(y_d)$ are the Hankel matrices of the input and output trajectories, respectively. These matrices span the trajectory space of the system and serve as a foundation for control synthesis

\subsection{Data-Driven MPC Formulations}
Using the Hankel-based representation, Berberich et al. formulate two variations of the data-driven MPC problem:

\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Nominal MPC:} Assumes noise-free measurements. Future trajectories are predicted using a linear combination of data-driven trajectories, and terminal equality constraints are included to guarantee exponential stability.

\item \textbf{Robust MPC:} Accounts for bounded additive measurement noise. A slack variable and corresponding regularization terms are introduced in the cost function, and robustness constraints are imposed to maintain feasibility.

\end{itemize}

Both schemes use a receding horizon strategy and optimize over trajectory weights rather than control inputs directly. These weights are used to reconstruct input-output trajectories from the Hankel matrix.

The upcoming sections detail how these theoretical constructs were implemented in code and validated through simulation.


\subsection{Behavioral Systems Theory}
\cite{berberich2021} uses behavioral systems theory to avoid explicit model identification. A key result is that a Hankel matrix constructed from a persistently exciting input-output trajectory can represent all future trajectories of the system.


