\item \textbf{Matplotlib:} Employed for data visualization, including input-output trajectories and system responses.

\end{itemize}

\subsection{Benchmark System: The Four-Tank Process}
The four-tank system is a well-known multivariable control benchmark with two inputs and two outputs. For this internship, a discrete-time linearized model of the system was used, given by:

\begin{align}
x_{k+1} &= A x_k + B u_k, \\
y_k &= C x_k + D u_k,
\end{align}

where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $x_k \in \mathbb{R}^4$ is the state vector (water levels in the tanks),
\item $u_k \in \mathbb{R}^2$ is the input vector (pump voltages),
\item $y_k \in \mathbb{R}^2$ is the output vector (measured levels),
\item $A \in \mathbb{R}^{4\times4}, B \in \mathbb{R}^{4\times2}, C \in \mathbb{R}^{2\times4}, D \in \mathbb{R}^{2\times2}$ are system matrices derived from linearization.
\end{itemize}

The specific matrices used in the implementation are:
\begin{equation*}
A = \begin{bmatrix}
0.921 & 0 & 0.041 & 0 \\
0 & 0.918 & 0 & 0.033 \\
0 & 0 & 0.924 & 0 \\
0 & 0 & 0 & 0.937
\end{bmatrix}, \quad
B = \begin{bmatrix}
0.017 & 0.001 \\
0.001 & 0.023 \\
0 & 0.061 \\
0.072 & 0
\end{bmatrix},
\end{equation*}

\begin{equation*}
C = \begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
0 & 1 & 0 & 0
\end{bmatrix}, \quad D = \begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 \end{bmatrix}.
\end{equation*}

The model was implemented in CasADi using symbolic variables and functions to enable efficient numerical simulation and optimization. Discretization was assumed to be consistent with the sampling rate used during the data collection and MPC execution.

