\section{Implementation}

This section presents the detailed implementation of the data-driven MPC algorithms, with emphasis on mathematical formulation, symbolic modeling in CasADi, and robustification mechanisms.

\subsection{Hankel Matrix Construction}
As discussed earlier, a Hankel matrix is constructed from the historical input-output data:
\begin{equation}
H_L(u_d, y_d) = \begin{bmatrix} H_L(u_d) \\ H_L(y_d) \end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{(m+p)L \times (T - L + 1)}
\end{equation}
where:
\begin{itemize}
\item $H_L(u_d) \in \mathbb{R}^{mL \times (T - L + 1)}$ is the input Hankel matrix,
\item $H_L(y_d) \in \mathbb{R}^{pL \times (T - L + 1)}$ is the output Hankel matrix,
\item $L = T_{ini} + T_{pred}$ is the total horizon (initial condition and prediction windows).
\end{itemize}

The collected trajectory is persistently exciting of order $L + n$ if the rank condition:
\begin{equation}
\text{rank}(H_{L+n}(u_d)) = m(L+n)
\end{equation}
is satisfied. This ensures that all future trajectories can be expressed using the data-driven basis.
\subsection{Optimization Variables and Cost Function}

In the nominal data-driven MPC formulation, the optimization problem is defined over three decision variables:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\alpha(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{N-L+1}$: the coefficient vector used to reconstruct trajectories from data,
    \item $\bar{u}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{mL}$: the predicted future input trajectory,
    \item $\bar{y}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{pL}$: the predicted future output trajectory.
\end{itemize}

The cost function is defined as:
\begin{equation}
J^*_L \left( u_{[t-n,t-1]}, y_{[t-n,t-1]} \right) =
\min_{\alpha(t),\, \bar{u}(t),\, \bar{y}(t)} 
\sum_{k=0}^{L-1} \ell\left( \bar{u}_k(t), \bar{y}_k(t) \right)
\end{equation}

subject to the following constraints:

\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[-n,L-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[-n,L-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
H_{L+n}(u^d) \\
H_{L+n}(y^d)
\end{bmatrix} \alpha(t) \tag{3b} \\[1em]
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[-n,-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[-n,-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
u_{[t-n,t-1]} \\
y_{[t-n,t-1]}
\end{bmatrix} \tag{3c} \\[1em]
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
u^s_n \\
y^s_n
\end{bmatrix} \tag{3d}
\end{align}

and the predicted trajectories must satisfy input/output constraints:
\begin{equation}
\bar{u}_k(t) \in \mathcal{U}, \quad \bar{y}_k(t) \in \mathcal{Y}, \quad k \in \mathbb{I}_{[0,L-1]} \tag{3e}
\end{equation}

This formulation ensures that future behavior is constrained to lie in the span of past data, initialized with current measurements, and guided to converge toward a desired steady-state operating point.


\subsection{Constraints}

Now we describe the concept of each constraint that defines the feasible set of the optimization problem. These constraints ensure that the predicted trajectories generated by the controller are consistent with the system's behavior, initialized correctly, and driven toward a desired equilibrium.

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Data consistency constraint:} 
    \begin{equation*}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \bar{u}_{[-n,L-1]}(t) \\
    \bar{y}_{[-n,L-1]}(t)
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
    H_{L+n}(u^d) \\
    H_{L+n}(y^d)
    \end{bmatrix} \alpha(t)
    \end{equation*}
    This enforces that the predicted input-output sequence lies in the space spanned by previously measured trajectories, consistent with the behavioral framework.

    \item \textbf{Initial condition constraint:}
    \begin{equation*}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \bar{u}_{[-n,-1]}(t) \\
    \bar{y}_{[-n,-1]}(t)
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
    u_{[t-n,t-1]} \\
    y_{[t-n,t-1]}
    \end{bmatrix}
    \end{equation*}
    This ensures that the predicted trajectory starts from the most recent system measurements and follows a physically plausible path.

    \item \textbf{Terminal constraint:}
    \begin{equation*}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \bar{u}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t) \\
    \bar{y}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t)
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
    u^s_n \\
    y^s_n
    \end{bmatrix}
    \end{equation*}
    This constraint drives the last part of the predicted trajectory to match a known steady-state input-output pair, which is essential for guaranteeing closed-loop exponential stability.

    \item \textbf{Input and output bounds:}
    \begin{equation*}
    \bar{u}_k(t) \in \mathcal{U}, \quad \bar{y}_k(t) \in \mathcal{Y}, \quad \forall k \in \mathbb{I}_{[0, L-1]}
    \end{equation*}
    These constraints ensure that the applied inputs and resulting outputs remain within physical or operational bounds imposed by the system.

    \item \textbf{Robustness constraint (robust formulation only):}
    \begin{equation*}
    \| \sigma_k \|_\infty \leq \epsilon \cdot (1 + \| \alpha(t) \|_1), \quad \forall k \in \mathbb{I}_{[0, L-1]}
    \end{equation*}
    This constraint limits the effect of measurement noise by bounding the slack variable $\sigma$ in terms of the magnitude of $\alpha(t)$, as proposed in \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop}.
\end{itemize}

