\section{Implementation}

This section presents the detailed implementation of the data-driven MPC algorithms.

\subsection{Hankel Matrix Construction}

The data-driven MPC framework leverages Hankel matrices to encode dynamic information from measured input-output trajectories. Rather than estimating a parametric model, the approach relies on behavioral system theory, where future trajectories are represented as linear combinations of previously observed data.

Let $\{u_k, y_k\}_{k=0}^{T-1}$ be a measured trajectory with $u_k \in \mathbb{R}^m$ and $y_k \in \mathbb{R}^p$. For a given horizon length $L$, the block Hankel matrices of depth $L$ are constructed as:

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_L(u) := 
\begin{bmatrix}
u_0 & u_1 & \cdots & u_{T-L} \\
u_1 & u_2 & \cdots & u_{T-L+1} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
u_{L-1} & u_L & \cdots & u_{T-1}
\end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{mL \times (T - L + 1)}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_L(y) := 
\begin{bmatrix}
y_0 & y_1 & \cdots & y_{T-L} \\
y_1 & y_2 & \cdots & y_{T-L+1} \\
\vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
y_{L-1} & y_L & \cdots & y_{T-1}
\end{bmatrix} \in \mathbb{R}^{pL \times (T - L + 1)}
\end{equation}

These matrices are horizontally stacked to form the combined Hankel matrix:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_L(u,y) := \begin{bmatrix}
\mathcal{H}_L(u) \\
\mathcal{H}_L(y)
\end{bmatrix}
\end{equation}

To enable prediction and control over the system, the collected input signal must be persistently exciting of order $L + n$, where $n$ is the assumed system order. This ensures that the Hankel matrix has full row rank and spans the space of admissible trajectories.

The Hankel matrix is partitioned into:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Past} window of length $T_{\mathrm{ini}}$, representing initial conditions
    \item \textbf{Future} window of length $T_{\mathrm{f}}$, used for optimization
\end{itemize}

The resulting formulation underpins the optimization problem solved in each MPC cycle, allowing trajectory prediction via data alone.




\subsection{Cost Function and Constraints Overview}

In data-driven MPC, the control task is posed as a constrained optimization problem over future input-output trajectories, which are implicitly defined by the Hankel data and a coefficient vector $\alpha$. Rather than optimizing over control inputs directly, the optimizer solves for $\alpha$ such that the predicted trajectory matches desired behavior.

Let $T_{\mathrm{f}}$ denote the prediction horizon. The predicted trajectory is constructed as:
\begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}
u_{\mathrm{ini}} \\\\
y_{\mathrm{ini}} \\\\
u_{\mathrm{pred}} \\\\
y_{\mathrm{pred}}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\mathcal{H}_{T_{\mathrm{ini}} + T_{\mathrm{f}}}(u, y) \cdot \alpha
\end{equation}
where $u_{\mathrm{ini}}, y_{\mathrm{ini}}$ are the known recent measurements (initial window), and $u_{\mathrm{pred}}, y_{\mathrm{pred}}$ are the predicted control inputs and outputs. The vector $\alpha$ contains the coefficients of the linear combination of trajectories from the data.

The cost function penalizes tracking error and control effort over the prediction horizon:
\begin{equation}
J(\alpha) = \sum_{k=0}^{T_{\mathrm{f}}-1} \left( \|y_k - y_{\mathrm{ref}}\|_Q^2 + \|u_k - u_{\mathrm{ref}}\|_R^2 \right)
\end{equation}
where $Q \succeq 0$ and $R \succ 0$ are tuning matrices, and $y_{\mathrm{ref}}, u_{\mathrm{ref}}$ are reference targets.

In the robust formulation, a slack variable $\sigma$ is introduced to account for bounded measurement noise, leading to a modified cost:
\begin{equation}
J_{\mathrm{robust}}(\alpha, \sigma) = J(\alpha) + \lambda_g \|\alpha\|_2^2 + \lambda_\sigma \|\sigma\|_2^2
\end{equation}

The optimization is constrained by:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Data constraint:} the predicted trajectory must lie in the column space of the Hankel matrix.
    \item \textbf{Initial condition consistency:} the initial $T_{\mathrm{ini}}$ steps must match measured inputs and outputs.
    \item \textbf{Terminal constraint:} the final segment of the trajectory should match the reference to ensure stability.
    \item \textbf{Input and output bounds:} $u_k \in \mathcal{U}$, $y_k \in \mathcal{Y}$ for all $k$.
    \item \textbf{Robustness constraint:} $\|\sigma_k\|_\infty \leq \epsilon (1 + \|\alpha\|_1)$ at each time step.
\end{itemize}

This formulation encapsulates the predictive control task using only data, without identifying a parametric model. In the next phase, this is implemented symbolically and solved at each control interval.




\subsection*{Implementation Summary}

The data-driven MPC controller was implemented using a modular design \cite{markovsky}, enabling flexibility between nominal and robust formulations. Each component, from Hankel matrix assembly to optimization, was embedded symbolically using CasADi, ensuring compatibility with both linear and nonlinear extensions. The optimization problem was solved using OSQP due to its speed and warm-starting capability, but the framework is general enough to accommodate solvers like IPOPT if required.

The formulations presented rely exclusively on measured data, circumventing the need for system identification. Importantly, the theoretical guarantees, including closed-loop stability and robust constraint satisfaction \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop}, were respected through the careful enforcement of terminal constraints, regularization, and persistency of excitation. This framework sets the foundation for the simulation and analysis of both nominal and noise-affected scenarios in the next section.



\section{Simulation and Results}

This section presents the results of closed-loop simulations conducted using both the nominal and robust data-driven MPC controllers. The goal is to validate the theoretical guarantees in practice and highlight the difference in behavior under measurement noise.

\subsection{Simulation Setup}

The test system is the discrete-time linearized four-tank process introduced earlier. The simulation is conducted over $180$ MPC cycles with a prediction horizon of $T_{\mathrm{f}} = 28$ and an initial condition window $T_{\mathrm{ini}} = 4$, yielding a total horizon length of $L = T_{\mathrm{ini}} + T_{\mathrm{f}} = 32$.

The input and output reference values, $u_{\mathrm{ref}}$ and $y_{\mathrm{ref}}$, were chosen to correspond to the system’s steady-state configuration. The cost function weights were selected as:
\begin{align*}
Q &= 2.8 \cdot I_2, \\\\
R &= 1.2 \cdot 10^{-4} \cdot I_2
\end{align*}
where $I_2$ is the $2 \times 2$ identity matrix, consistent with the system’s two output and two input channels, to penalize output deviation and input usage, respectively. Input bounds were enforced at $\pm 9.5$, while output constraints were left inactive in the nominal case.

To ensure data richness, the Hankel matrix was built from $N = 420$ samples of PRBS-driven input-output data. The PRBS signals were scaled and validated for persistency of excitation by confirming the rank condition:
\begin{equation}
\text{rank}(\mathcal{H}_{L+n}(u)) = m(L + n)
\end{equation}

Each simulation consisted of three phases:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Initialization:} Build Hankel matrix using collected data and define solver settings.
    \item \textbf{Execution:} At each step, solve the optimization problem using current measurements and apply the first input from the optimal predicted sequence.
    \item \textbf{Update:} Shift the measurement window and warm-start the solver with previous values.
\end{enumerate}

In the robust case, additive measurement noise was applied to the output signals, bounded by:
\begin{equation}
\|\varepsilon_k\|_{\infty} \leq \epsilon = 1.8 \times 10^{-3}
\end{equation}
Regularization weights were tuned to:
\begin{align*}
\lambda_g &= 0.12 / \epsilon, \\\\
\lambda_\sigma &= 1.2 \times 10^3
\end{align*}
to maintain feasibility and mitigate noise effects.




\subsection{Nominal MPC Results}

The first scenario considers the idealized case in which the collected and real-time measurement data are noise-free. The nominal data-driven MPC problem follows the structure of Problem (3) in \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop} and relies on a single measured trajectory for system parametrization.

At each time step $t$, the optimization problem solved follows the nominal data-driven MPC formulation:

\begin{equation}
\min_{\alpha(t),\, \bar{u}(t),\, \bar{y}(t)} 
\sum_{k=0}^{L-1} \left( \| \bar{u}_k(t) - u_{\mathrm{ref}} \|_R^2 + \| \bar{y}_k(t) - y_{\mathrm{ref}} \|_Q^2 \right)
\label{eq:nominal_cost}
\end{equation}

subject to the following constraints:

\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[-n,L-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[-n,L-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\label{16}
\begin{bmatrix}
\mathcal{H}_{L+n}(u^d) \\
\mathcal{H}_{L+n}(y^d)
\end{bmatrix} \alpha(t) \\[1em]
\label{17}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[-n,-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[-n,-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
u_{[t-n,t-1]} \\
y_{[t-n,t-1]}
\end{bmatrix} \\[1em]
\label{18}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
u^s_n \\
y^s_n
\end{bmatrix}
\end{align}


\noindent
The predicted trajectories \( \bar{u}_k(t) \in \mathcal{U}, \, \bar{y}_k(t) \in \mathcal{Y} \) for all \( k \in \mathbb{I}_{[0,L-1]} \) must also satisfy input and output bounds, and the structure of the terminal constraint ensures convergence toward the desired steady-state reference.

Constraints \eqref{16} and \eqref{17} ensure that the predicted trajectories are both consistent with the behavioral model and initialized with actual measurements. The terminal constraint \eqref{18}, imposed over $n$ time steps, ensures exponential convergence to the setpoint.

In simulation, the controller was applied over 180 cycles. Output signals converged to their references rapidly and without oscillations, validating the nominal DDMPC framework’s ability to stabilize the system using only data.

