\subsection{Robust MPC Results}

In practical settings, output measurements are often corrupted by bounded noise, which, if not properly addressed, can lead to infeasibility or degraded tracking performance in nominal DDMPC. To account for this, the controller was reconfigured using the robust formulation described in \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop, overview-DDMPC}, based on Problem (4) in \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop}.

\subsection{Robust MPC Results}

To address bounded measurement noise, the robust data-driven MPC formulation includes an additional slack variable $\sigma(t)$ and modifies the cost and constraints accordingly. This allows the controller to maintain feasibility and stability guarantees even when output data is perturbed.

The optimization problem at time $t$ is given by:

\begin{align}
\min_{\alpha(t),\, \bar{u}(t),\, \bar{y}(t),\, \sigma(t)} \quad 
& \sum_{k=0}^{L-1} \ell\left( \bar{u}_k(t), \bar{y}_k(t) \right) 
+ \lambda_\alpha \bar{\epsilon} \| \alpha(t) \|_2^2 
+ \lambda_\sigma \| \sigma(t) \|_2^2 \label{eq:robust_cost}
\end{align}

subject to the constraints:

\begin{align}
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}(t) \\
\bar{y}(t) + \sigma(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
H_{L+n}(u^d) \\
H_{L+n}(y^d)
\end{bmatrix} \alpha(t) \tag{4b} \\[1em]
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[-n,-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[-n,-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
u_{[t-n,t-1]} \\
y_{[t-n,t-1]}
\end{bmatrix} \tag{4c} \\[1em]
\begin{bmatrix}
\bar{u}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t) \\
\bar{y}_{[L-n,L-1]}(t)
\end{bmatrix}
&=
\begin{bmatrix}
u^s_n \\
y^s_n
\end{bmatrix}, \quad \bar{u}_k(t) \in \mathcal{U} \tag{4d}
\end{align}

and the noise variable is constrained by:
\begin{equation}
\| \sigma_k(t) \|_\infty \leq \bar{\epsilon} \cdot (1 + \| \alpha(t) \|_1), \quad \forall k \in \mathbb{I}_{[0, L-1]} \tag{4e}
\end{equation}

This structure ensures that the controller can accommodate bounded disturbances while preserving the convergence and constraint satisfaction guarantees. The regularization weights $ \lambda_\alpha $ and $ \lambda_\sigma $ penalize complexity in trajectory selection and noise deviation, respectively.


The regularization terms in the cost penalize overly flexible reconstructions of the trajectory that might otherwise fit noise. As shown in \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop}, this yields practical exponential stability, meaning the system converges to a small neighborhood around the reference depending on the noise level $\epsilon$.

Simulations were conducted using the same prediction and initialization horizons as in the nominal case. Measurement noise was injected as bounded additive perturbations:
\[
\|\varepsilon_k\|_{\infty} \leq \epsilon = 1.8 \times 10^{-3}
\]
and the regularization weights were tuned as:
\[
\lambda_\alpha = \frac{0.12}{\epsilon}, \quad \lambda_\sigma = 1.2 \times 10^3
\]

The closed-loop response confirms robust performance: the outputs remained close to reference values, with tracking error bounded as expected.

As in the nominal case, the evolution of input and output measurements across control iterations revealed consistent improvements in tracking. Despite the presence of measurement noise, each successive plot shows a tightening of the response around the reference. This reflects the robust controller’s ability to attenuate disturbances and converge within a guaranteed bound.

