\subsection{Solver Performance Analysis}

Figure~\ref{fig:solver_time_comparison} compares the per-iteration solver times for the nominal and robust data-driven MPC schemes. Both scenarios were tested over 180 cycles using warm-started OSQP solvers.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{subfigure}{0.48\textwidth}
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Solver_Time_per_Iteration.png}
\caption{Nominal DDMPC (No Output Disturbance)}
\label{fig:solver_time_nominal}
  \end{subfigure}
  \hfill
  \begin{subfigure}{0.48\textwidth}
    \includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{Solver_Time_per_Iteration_d.png}
    \caption{Robust DDMPC (With Output Disturbance)}
    \label{fig:solver_time_robust}
  \end{subfigure}
  \caption{Comparison of MPC solver time per iteration. Robust DDMPC introduces higher variability and computational load due to slack variable handling and noise-related constraint complexity.}
  \label{fig:solver_time_comparison}
\end{figure}

In the nominal case (Figure~\ref{fig:solver_time_nominal}), the majority of solver times are below 50 ms, with periodic spikes up to 3500 ms. These spikes likely correspond to shifts in active constraints or infrequent warm-start failures. Still, the solver maintains real-time feasibility for most of the horizon.

In contrast, the robust MPC case (Figure~\ref{fig:solver_time_robust}) exhibits significantly higher variability, with multiple solver spikes exceeding 10,000 ms and one peak surpassing 20,000 ms. This increase is attributed to the added complexity from:
\begin{itemize}
  \item The introduction of the slack variable $\sigma$ in the optimization,
  \item Noise-affected constraints that are more sensitive to feasibility margins,
  \item The higher condition number induced by regularization balancing $\|\alpha\|$ and $\|\sigma\|$.
\end{itemize}

These findings highlight an important trade-off: while robust DDMPC improves feasibility and tracking reliability in the presence of noise, it incurs a computational cost. In real-world applications, further tuning or solver acceleration techniques may be necessary to meet tight timing constraints.









\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

This internship explored the implementation, simulation, and analysis of data-driven model predictive control (DDMPC) schemes. Using only input-output data, a controller was built based on Hankel matrix representations, following the behavioral systems theory approach presented in \cite{berberich2021, DDMPC-closed-loop, overview-DDMPC}. Both nominal and robust DDMPC variants were implemented, tested on a linearized four-tank system, and evaluated in terms of performance, stability, and computational efficiency.

\subsection*{Implementation Challenges and Reflections}

A central technical component of the work was the symbolic construction of the optimization problem using CasADi. While CasADi provides a powerful toolchain for modeling and solving optimal control problems, it presented specific implementation difficulties:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Symbolic reshaping and concatenation of trajectory vectors was error-prone, especially in defining consistent indexing over input/output sequences.
  \item Handling warm-started variables and dual multipliers in OSQP required strict alignment between CasADi-generated constraints and solver configuration.
  \item CasADi's internal memory graph and expression trees made it difficult to debug subtle shape mismatches or unintended symbolic duplications.
\end{itemize}

These challenges led to repeated refinement of the solver interface and optimization logic, ultimately deepening the understanding of both CasADi’s symbolic backend and MPC design requirements.

\subsection*{Behavioral Modeling vs. Explicit Dynamics}

Another valuable insight came from comparing the behavioral, data-driven approach to the known physical model of the four-tank system. Since the linearized state-space model was available (via known $A$, $B$, $C$, $D$ matrices), it was possible to validate whether the Hankel-based trajectory predictions aligned with true system behavior.

Empirical results indicated that:
\begin{itemize}
  \item When the excitation data satisfied the persistency condition and noise was minimal, the Hankel-based predictor reconstructed future trajectories accurately, confirming theoretical expectations.
  \item Under high noise or insufficient data richness, the predictive accuracy of the behavioral model degraded noticeably compared to the known parametric model.
\end{itemize}

This provided strong justification for both the strengths and the limitations of DDMPC — it works well within the assumptions, but its success depends critically on data quality and excitation design.

\subsection*{Summary of Contributions}

The project demonstrated the complete implementation of DDMPC in Python using CasADi, including:
\begin{itemize}
  \item Construction of data-driven predictors using Hankel matrices.
  \item Implementation of nominal and robust MPC schemes with terminal constraints.
  \item Simulation-based validation on a multivariable system.
  \item Performance diagnostics including solver time profiling, convergence visualization, and trajectory accuracy.
\end{itemize}

In doing so, the internship successfully bridged theoretical methods from cutting-edge literature with hands-on control implementation and highlighted key design decisions necessary for practical deployment.

\subsection*{Future Work}

Several directions for future investigation and extension are clear:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Nonlinear DDMPC:} Extend the controller to nonlinear systems using locally updated Hankel matrices and affine approximations, as suggested in \cite{DDMPC-closed-loop}.
  \item \textbf{Online data adaptation:} Implement schemes to update the trajectory data window online while preserving persistency of excitation.
  \item \textbf{Constraint tightening:} Apply robust constraint satisfaction techniques (e.g., tube MPC or set-invariant tightening) for bounded-noise scenarios.
  \item \textbf{Model-augmented DDMPC:} Explore hybrid approaches that combine data-driven predictors with partial physical models to improve prediction reliability.
  \item \textbf{Experimental validation:} Deploy the controller on hardware (e.g., a physical four-tank system) to evaluate real-time feasibility, sensor noise, and actuator saturation effects.
\end{itemize}

Overall, the internship provided a deep practical understanding of data-driven control, strengthened coding and optimization skills, and served as a robust introduction to the research frontier of model-free MPC.




